{
  "verdict": "divergent",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "major",
      "description": "With `Q N` meaning “N is conscious,” `N.D9 → Q N` says every feedforward system is conscious. The intended premise is `N.D9 → ¬ Q N`. Consequently, the witness to `∃ N, Q N` need not contrast with any feedforward system. For example, take `X = Y = Unit` and let both `Q` and `c4` always return `True`: the hypotheses hold, but there is no mismatch. Reading `Q` as “unconscious” does not fix this, since the existential would then assert the existence of an unconscious system rather than a conscious one."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "The behavior compared is that of a deterministic, finite-node system from a fixed initial state on input lists of length at most `T`. P3 speaks of identical input-output functions without that cutoff. Requiring inference to agree even when systems agree only through `T` is a stronger invariance condition."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "`c4` can be any predicate on the entire bounded behavior function, not necessarily an inference obtainable from a physical measurement or report. Thus the witness is a system with conflicting yes/no verdicts, the model's proxy for Kleiner and Hoel's observation at which a falsification occurs."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Apart from the stated hypotheses, `Q` is arbitrary. Even with the polarity corrected, it need not depend only on how the elements interact; the premises capture a contrast sufficient for the argument rather than characterize causal structure theories."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The conclusion correctly expresses disagreement without assigning fault. The reversed feedforward premise, however, permits verdicts that agree everywhere, so the intended mismatch does not follow."
}
